              },
              {
                ":answer/id": "4026b447-0d95-56cd-af37-471328d81281",
                ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
                ":answer/value": "-\\frac{4}{3}"
              }
            ],
            ":answer-field/correct": [
              ":answer/id",
              "4bf532b2-930d-5041-aafb-acc26b8eef68"
            ]
          }
        ],
        ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -3 \\\\ k & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}|)+((3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}|)+((-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (0)-(-2)\\cdot (-2)))+((3)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (0)-(-2)\\cdot (-2)))+((-3)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (-2)-(0)\\cdot (-2)))\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot (-4))+((3)\\cdot (-4))+((-3)\\cdot (-2k))\\\\ &=(-4)+(-12)+(6k)\\\\ &=6k-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6k-16&=0\\\\ 6k&=16\\\\ k&=\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{8}{3}.$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy"
