      "question": {
        ":question/id": "b19b3600-3782-5719-8b4b-54b70445653d",
        ":question/problem": "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=\u27e81,0,-1\u27e9$, $b=\u27e82,-2,0\u27e9$, and $c=\u27e81,-2,k\u27e9$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/id": "75ce68ff-001a-5d18-89b6-4460cfebbbf2",
            ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
            ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
            ":answer-field/choices": [
              {
                ":answer/id": "0a1e0999-560e-596f-a43f-4cc139aad91e",
                ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
                ":answer/value": "1"
              }
            ],
            ":answer-field/correct": [
              ":answer/id",
              "0a1e0999-560e-596f-a43f-4cc139aad91e"
            ]
          }
        ],
        ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & k\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{aligned}|)+((0)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}|)+((-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (k)-(0)\\cdot (-2)))+((0)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (k)-(0)\\cdot (1)))+((-1)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (-2)-(-2)\\cdot (1)))\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot (-2k))+((0)\\cdot (2k))+((-1)\\cdot (-2))\\\\ &=(-2k)+(0)+(2)\\\\ &=2-2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-2k&=0\\\\ -2k&=-2\\\\ k&=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=1.$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy"
      },
