              "e67af7bd-0910-5792-904d-394262291e82"
            ]
          }
        ],
        ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & k \\\\ -3 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & k \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}|)+((-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & k \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}|)+((0)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((2)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (-2)-(k)\\cdot (-1)))+((-2)\\cdot ((3)\\cdot (-2)-(k)\\cdot (-3)))+((0)\\cdot ((3)\\cdot (-1)-(0)\\cdot (-3)))\\\\ &=((2)\\cdot (k))+((-2)\\cdot (3k-6))+((0)\\cdot (-3))\\\\ &=(2k)+(12-6k)+(0)\\\\ &=12-4k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-4k&=0\\\\ -4k&=-12\\\\ k&=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=3.$",
