            ":answer-field/correct": [
              ":answer/id",
              "76c5b4cb-6d55-5b36-bc09-4860517acd1d"
            ]
          }
        ],
        ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ k & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|)+((1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ k & -2\\end{aligned}|)+((-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ k & 0\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (-2)-(1)\\cdot (0)))+((1)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (-2)-(1)\\cdot (k)))+((-1)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (0)-(0)\\cdot (k)))\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot (0))+((1)\\cdot (-k-4))+((-1)\\cdot (0))\\\\ &=(0)+(-k-4)+(0)\\\\ &=-k-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-k-4&=0\\\\ -k&=4\\\\ k&=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-4.$",
