        ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ k & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|)+((-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ k & 1\\end{aligned}|)+((2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ k & 0\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot ((3)\\cdot (1)-(-1)\\cdot (0)))+((-2)\\cdot ((-1)\\cdot (1)-(-1)\\cdot (k)))+((2)\\cdot ((-1)\\cdot (0)-(3)\\cdot (k)))\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot (3))+((-2)\\cdot (k-1))+((2)\\cdot (-3k))\\\\ &=(0)+(2-2k)+(-6k)\\\\ &=2-8k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-8k&=0\\\\ -8k&=-2\\\\ k&=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{1}{4}.$",
