        ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}6 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 7 \\\\ k & -2 & -3\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((6)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 7 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}|)+((7)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 \\\\ k & -3\\end{aligned}|)+((4)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ k & -2\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((6)\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (-3)-(7)\\cdot (-2)))+((7)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (-3)-(7)\\cdot (k)))+((4)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (-2)-(-2)\\cdot (k)))\\\\ &=((6)\\cdot (20))+((7)\\cdot (-7k-6))+((4)\\cdot (2k-4))\\\\ &=(120)+(-49k-42)+(8k-16)\\\\ &=62-41k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}62-41k&=0\\\\ -41k&=-62\\\\ k&=\\frac{62}{41}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{62}{41}.$",
